Handle the unpaired last element when the array has an odd length:

```java
public static int[] shuffle(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int mid = n / 2;
    int[] result = new int[n];

    for (int j = 0; j < mid; j++) {
        result[j * 2] = nums[j];
        result[j * 2 + 1] = nums[j + mid];
    }

    // The second half has one extra element when n is odd.
    if (n % 2 != 0) {
        result[n - 1] = nums[n - 1];
    }

    return result;
}
```

For example:

```java
int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
```

produces:

```java
{1, 3, 2, 4, 5}
```

An alternative that handles odd and even arrays uniformly is:

```java
public static int[] shuffle(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int mid = n / 2;
    int[] result = new int[n];

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (i < mid) {
            result[i * 2] = nums[i];
        } else {
            result[(i - mid) * 2 + 1] = nums[i];
        }
    }

    return result;
}
